course: dictionary
lesson: 15042
title: Retention: keeping hold of things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Retention is a formal word that comes from the verb retain, which means to keep. Its main meaning is keeping something, or keeping it from being lost. «Good memory retention helps Maya remember every spelling she learns.» Here retention means how well she keeps facts in her mind.
explain: The context tells you what is being kept. «The sandy soil has poor water retention, so the rain runs straight through.» The soil is not holding the water. «Because of retention, Sam stayed in Year 4 for another year.» In UK schools, retention means a pupil is kept back a year.
explain: Test your meaning in the sentence. If "keeping" or "holding on to" still makes sense, you are close. Look at the word before retention, such as memory, water or heat, to see what is being kept.
fact: Retention means keeping something, or stopping it from being lost.
fact: Retention comes from the verb «retain», which means to keep.
fact: «Memory retention» means how much you keep in your memory.
fact: «Water retention» means the body holds on to too much water.
fact: In UK schools, retention means keeping a pupil back a year.
exercise: «Good memory retention helps you remember the spellings you learn.» What does memory retention mean here? | How many books you own | How fast you can run | How loudly you can speak | *How well you keep facts in your mind
exercise: «The sandy soil has poor water retention, so the rain runs straight through.» What does water retention mean? | Washing the soil away | Sending water up into the sky | Painting the sand blue | *Keeping water in, so it does not run away
exercise: «Because of retention, Sam stayed in Year 4 for another year.» What does retention mean in this sentence? | A new school uniform | *A pupil being kept back a year | A prize for good work | A trip to the museum
test: «Keeping the heat in helps the house stay warm. This is called heat retention.» What does heat retention mean? | *Keeping heat in, so it does not escape | Making things colder | Heat that comes only from the sun | Cooking food quickly
test: Lily says retention means losing things for ever. Is she right? | No, it means a loud noise | Yes, it means losing something for ever | Yes, but only in science lessons | *No, it means keeping something or keeping it from being lost
exam: «The doctor checked for water retention in Amira's legs.» What does water retention mean? | Her legs were dry and cracked | She had new boots on her feet | *Her body was holding too much water | She had been swimming in a pool
